# Cloudflare Pages redirect rules. Astro's `redirects` config sends / to /en/,
# so the root is handled by the build.
#
# The old site's URLs are not typed here. scripts/legacy-redirects.mjs appends
# them to dist/_redirects after every build, from a map of old page to new page
# plus the old slugs in legacy-export/normalized, and fails the build if any of
# them would point at a page that does not exist. Edit the map there.
#
# Hand-written rules can still go above this line; the generated block is
# appended after them.

# Dutch, off the site while it is retranslated (Kamindu, 2026-10-17). Every
# /nl/ address answers a **302** to the English homepage - temporary, never a
# 301, which a browser would keep long after Dutch comes back.
#
# The pages themselves are still built and still carry their hreflang, which
# is what `scripts/legacy-redirects.mjs` reads to find the Dutch target of
# every old-site rule; a rule is followed whether or not a file matches it
# (Cloudflare Pages, "Redirects"), so the files are simply never served.
# Dutch was already out of the switcher and out of search - see `HELD_BACK`
# in `src/lib/locales.ts`, which names the three places this state lives.
#
# Two notes. The static rules below and in the generated block are matched
# before a splat rule, so an old Dutch URL takes two hops while this is on
# (/nl/contact/ → /nl/over/team/ → /en/); it lands in the right place, but
# `legacy-redirects.mjs --check` reports those rules as landing on a 302.
# And /preview/nl/… is untouched: the drafts preview still renders Dutch, so
# the retranslation can be read before it goes anywhere.
#
# To put Dutch back: delete these three lines (and see locales.ts).
/nl    /en/   302
/nl/   /en/   302
/nl/*  /en/   302

